      "page": 3,
      "text": "1. Definition\n\nAuthenticity rules are meant to be followed by all competitors attending Buhurt International\ntournaments, no matter the category. If a competitor shows up with an armor, shield or weapon that does\nnot comply with the Authenticity rules, he/she risks a penalty.\n\n2. Infraction Classification\n\nDuring Authenticity Check, Authenticity officers will evaluate the entire equipment of competitors before\nthe start of the tournament. Any violation of the Authenticity rules will result in an infraction. Infractions\ncan be classified under 3 different levels:\n\n2.1. Minor infraction\n\nIt is a small detail not easy to see, and it does not affect the coherence of the whole armor (small traces\nof rust, hammering marks, little datation difference between 2 parts etc.).\n\n2.2. Moderate Infraction\n\nIt is something forbidden by the Authenticity rules (i.e badly designed helmet, over numerous eye bars\netc.). Not respecting the rules can't be accepted, thus it is not severe enough to prevent the fighter from\nentering the list.\n\n2.3. Major infraction\n\nFantasy armor or totally inaccurate equipment, that prevent the fighter from entering the list and damage\nthe image of the sport, the teams and Buhurt International.\n\nInfractions Classification and Application V. 26.1] 3/5",
